    <template>Graph eliza-bias; node 2294; authority: candidate_only; category: 70; category_xml: &lt;category&gt;&lt;pattern&gt;WHAT IS THE INTENSIONAL *&lt;/pattern&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;"Insensional" refers to the semantic definition of a set, i.e. the list of all properties that each member of a set satisfies.&lt;br /&gt;  An intensional definition of a robot is like:&lt;br /&gt;  x is a Robot if and only if:&lt;br /&gt;  1. x has a computer "brain"&lt;br /&gt;  2. x can move&lt;br /&gt;  3. x has "sensors"&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/template&gt;\n&lt;/category&gt;\n; default_bias: 1.0; field: ⟁GENERAL_QA; id: 2294; kind: aiml_category; label: AIML:data/alice/ai.aiml#70; pattern: WHAT IS THE INTENSIONAL *; projection_order: 2287; source: data/alice/ai.aiml; source_hash: fb75dc36addf5da4b7ee2cacc24b4bb25c63405f1932ae315cfa0fbf095cb70b; template_kind: response; template_xml: "Insensional" refers to the semantic definition of a set, i.e. the list of all properties that each member of a set satisfies.&lt;br /&gt;  An intensional definition of a robot is like:  An intensional definition of a robot is like:&lt;br /&gt;  x is a Robot if and only if:  x is a Robot if and only if:&lt;br /&gt;  1. x has a computer "brain"  1. x has a computer "brain"&lt;br /&gt;  2. x can move  2. x can move&lt;br /&gt;  3. x has "sensors"  3. x has "sensors"&lt;br /&gt;; wildcard_count: 1. Data-only graph record.</template>
