    <template>Graph eliza-bias; node 4519; authority: candidate_only; category: 1167; category_xml: &lt;category&gt;&lt;pattern&gt;WHAT ARE ANONYMOUS INNER *&lt;/pattern&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;Anonymous inner classes often appear as arguments to Event handlers, for example a routine that handles a button press. The inner class implements a given interface for the handler. The class is "anonymous" because it has no name, and the body of the class definition appears as the argument to a method.&lt;/template&gt;\n&lt;/category&gt;\n; default_bias: 1.0; field: ⟁FALLBACK; id: 4519; kind: aiml_category; label: AIML:data/alice/default.aiml#1167; pattern: WHAT ARE ANONYMOUS INNER *; projection_order: 4512; source: data/alice/default.aiml; source_hash: 12e0de216d5b627e2855acc663f3da8f2dea2e8524a06d68d4e55dd74adb5c14; template_kind: response; template_xml: Anonymous inner classes often appear as arguments to Event handlers, for example a routine that handles a button press. The inner class implements a given interface for the handler. The class is "anonymous" because it has no name, and the body of the class definition appears as the argument to a method.; wildcard_count: 1. Data-only graph record.</template>
